course: dictionary
lesson: 12797
title: Electrocution: a danger word
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Electrocution is a noun for being killed or badly hurt by an electric shock. You can spot clues in the word itself: it starts with «electric», so it is about electricity. «There is a serious risk of electrocution if you touch a live wire.» Serious and risk tell you this is very dangerous.
explain: Look at the sentences around the word. «Never play near water or wires, because of the risk of electrocution.» Water and wires are the clues: they show where an electric shock could happen. Ask yourself what would fit in the gap, then try your meaning in the sentence instead of the hard word.
explain: Use the swap check. «There is a serious risk of an electric shock if you touch a live wire» still makes sense, so you have the right meaning. Live means the electricity is flowing and can hurt you.
fact: Electrocution means being killed or badly hurt by an electric shock.
fact: «Risk of electrocution» means there is a chance of an electric shock that could hurt you very badly.
fact: Water and electricity are a dangerous mix, so keep electrical things such as hairdryers away from baths and sinks.
fact: Live wires and pylons are places where electrocution can happen, so never touch a live wire and never climb a pylon.
fact: Electrocution also has a separate, grown-up meaning in law and US history, about executing a criminal, which is not used in school safety lessons.
exercise: «There is a risk of electrocution if you touch a live wire.» What does electrocution mean? | Being cold and wet from the rain | A loud noise from a pylon | *Being badly hurt by an electric shock | A sweet made in a factory
exercise: «Keep the hairdryer away from the bath to avoid electrocution.» Why is this important? | So the bath water gets hot faster | So the hairdryer stays warm | So the bath stays dry | *An electric shock could badly hurt you
exercise: «The sign on the pylon warned people about the danger of electrocution.» What did the sign warn about? | Cheap ice creams | A football match | *An electric shock that could hurt badly | A lost dog
test: «Tom's teacher said never to play near water and wires, because of the risk of electrocution.» What is the risk? | Getting lost on the way home | Getting too much homework | Getting a sunburn | *An electric shock that could badly hurt him
test: Which phrase means the same as «danger of electrocution»? | A chance of winning a prize | *A chance of a dangerous electric shock | A chance of a sunny day | A chance of a long walk
exam: «Electrocution» most nearly means: | Being given a prize for good work | *Being killed or badly hurt by an electric shock | Feeling very tired after a long day | Being lost in a dark wood
